course: world
lesson: 37
title: Germs, hygiene and vaccines
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Germs are living things too small to see without a microscope. Most bacteria are harmless or even helpful, but some bacteria and viruses get into the body and make us ill.
explain: Germs spread through coughs and sneezes, dirty hands, dirty food and water, and some through insect bites. Washing hands with soap for about 20 seconds, covering a cough and cooking food well stop many of them.
explain: The body fights back: white blood cells attack the germs and make antibodies that fit one kind of germ like a key fits a lock. Afterwards the body remembers that germ and beats it quickly next time.
explain: A vaccine teaches the body this lesson safely: it shows the body a weakened, killed or harmless part of a germ, so the body makes antibodies without getting really ill. When many people are vaccinated, the germ cannot spread, which protects even those who cannot be vaccinated.
explain: Antibiotics are medicines that kill bacteria, but they do not work against viruses such as the cold or the flu.
fact: Germs are tiny living things that can only be seen with a microscope.
fact: Bacteria and viruses are two kinds of germs.
fact: Most bacteria are harmless, and some, such as those in our gut, are helpful.
fact: Germs spread through coughs, sneezes, touch, dirty food and dirty water.
fact: Washing hands with soap for about 20 seconds removes most germs.
fact: Soap breaks apart the oily layer that sticks germs to the skin, so water can wash them away.
fact: White blood cells fight germs and make antibodies.
fact: After an illness the body often remembers the germ and fights it quickly next time.
fact: A vaccine trains the body to fight a germ without getting really ill.
fact: There are vaccines against illnesses such as measles, polio and flu.
fact: Antibiotics kill bacteria but do not work against viruses.
fact: Colds and flu are caused by viruses.
fact: Doctors found that washing hands saves lives; Ignaz Semmelweis showed it in a hospital in the 1840s, where fewer mothers died of infection once doctors washed their hands.
exercise: What are germs? | Tiny living things too small to see | Tiny bits of dust that fall off the floor | Small insects we can see easily on food | Drops of water floating about in the air
exercise: Which two are kinds of germs? | Bacteria and viruses | Vitamins and minerals | Muscles and tendons | Proteins and fats
exercise: Which is true about bacteria? | Most are harmless and some are helpful | All bacteria make us ill | Bacteria are as big as ants | Bacteria live only in hospitals and dirty bins
exercise: How long should we wash our hands with soap? | About 20 seconds | About 1 second | About 10 minutes | Only until they are wet
exercise: How does soap help to remove germs? | It breaks the oily layer that sticks germs to the skin | It feeds the germs until they leave | It makes the hands much warmer than the germs can stand to be | It colours the germs so we can see them
exercise: Which body cells fight germs? | White blood cells | Red blood cells | Bone cells | Hair cells
exercise: What do white blood cells make to fight one kind of germ? | Antibodies | Vitamins | Saliva | Tendons
exercise: What does a vaccine do? | It trains the body to fight a germ without real illness | It kills every germ in the body at once, good and bad alike | It gives the body extra blood | It makes the body grow faster
exercise: What kind of germ causes the common cold? | A virus | A bacterium that antibiotics kill | A vitamin | A worm
exercise: Which germs do antibiotics kill? | Bacteria | Viruses | Both bacteria and viruses equally | Neither, they only stop pain
exercise: How do germs often spread? | Through coughs, sneezes and dirty hands | Through reading books aloud to each other | Through just looking at other people's faces | Through cold weather alone, without any person
exercise: Why should we cover a cough or sneeze? | To stop tiny drops carrying germs to others | To keep our mouth warm | To make the cough louder | To stop our lungs from emptying out all the air
exercise: Why should meat be cooked well? | Heat kills germs that may be in it | Cooking adds extra vitamins to the meat | Raw meat has no protein in it at all | Cooked meat is lighter to carry home
exercise: Why does a person who had chickenpox usually not get it again? | The body remembers the germ and fights it fast | The germ dies out in the world forever | Chickenpox only comes once a century | The spots protect the skin
exercise: How does vaccinating many people help those who cannot be vaccinated? | The germ cannot spread from person to person | The vaccine floats through the air from the others to them | They become immune by standing nearby | It does not help them at all
test: Sam has a cold and asks for antibiotics. What should the doctor say? | Antibiotics do not work on cold viruses | Antibiotics cure every illness at once | Antibiotics turn a cold into flu | Take double antibiotics for faster healing
test: A class washes their hands before lunch. Fewer children get tummy bugs. Why? | Fewer germs reach their mouths with the food | Soap gives them extra vitamins | Washing the hands makes their stomachs stronger and tougher | The water warms their food
test: Which is true? | A vaccine shows the body a harmless form of a germ | A vaccine is made of white blood cells | A vaccine makes you ill so badly that you learn | A vaccine kills all helpful bacteria too
test: Why do antibodies work against only one kind of germ? | Each fits its own germ like a key fits one lock | Each one is far too small to grab any other germs | Antibodies are made in the stomach | Antibodies only work at night
test: If nobody washed their hands, what would most likely happen? | More people would fall ill | Germs would die out from loneliness | People would become stronger and fitter | Nothing would change for anyone
test: A doctor in the 1840s told other doctors to wash their hands. What happened? | Fewer mothers in his hospital died of infection | More people caught colds | The hospital ran out of water | Nothing changed
test: Yoghurt is full of live bacteria. Is it safe to eat? | Yes, many bacteria are harmless or helpful | No, all bacteria are dangerous | Only if it is boiled for a long time first | Only if eaten with antibiotics
test: A girl sneezes into her elbow instead of her hand. Why is that a good idea? | Her hands will not spread the germs to things she touches | Elbows make sneezes quieter for the lungs | Germs cannot live anywhere on the body | Hands are needed to cool the sneeze
test: Which illness can be prevented with a vaccine? | Measles | A broken bone | Tooth decay | Short sight
test: why bother with soap, water washes germs off anyway innit | Soap breaks the oily layer holding germs, so water can wash them away | Water alone removes all germs | Soap kills germs only when the water is very hot, otherwise it does nothing | Soap just makes the hands smell nice
test: got the flu jab at school, why'd that help | A vaccine trains the body to fight the germ without getting really ill | It puts the flu in your body forever | It kills all the germs in the room | It makes you sleep through the flu
test: my mum's mate says take antibiotics for a cold. true? | No, colds are viruses and antibiotics only kill bacteria | Yes, antibiotics cure every illness | Yes, they work best on colds and stop them in a single day | No, colds are cured by eating sweets
test: Ben says all bacteria are bad for you. right? | No, most are harmless, some even help | Yes, every single bacterium makes us ill | Yes, but only the ones that live in water | No, bacteria are not alive at all, just dust
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the majority of bacteria? | Most are harmless, and some are beneficial | All are harmful and cause disease in people | All are viruses of a larger size | None live within the human body
exam: Identify the substances that are effective against bacteria but not against viruses. | Antibiotics | Vaccines | Soap and water alone | Vitamins
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a vaccine? | It trains the body to combat a germ without serious illness | It destroys all bacteria within the body | It cures a cold once contracted | It replaces the white blood cells
exam: State the approximate duration recommended for the washing of hands with soap. | Twenty seconds | Two seconds | Two minutes | Twenty minutes
